      section: "Section 1 · The door",
      body: `A closed profession rarely opens with a policy. It opens with someone handing one person a short, revocable contract and waiting to see what happens.

**Jayne Kennedy, CBS, 1978.** Kennedy was born in Washington, D.C., in 1951 and raised in Ohio, where she won Miss Ohio USA in 1970. From 1978 to 1980 she co-anchored *The NFL Today* on CBS, succeeding Phyllis George. She was the first Black woman to co-anchor a national network sports show. Note the wording: George held that desk before her, so "first woman" is wrong and "first Black woman" is right. Precision is not pedantry here. It is the difference between a claim that survives a fact-check and one that hands a critic a free win.

**How she got there.** Not on a multi-year deal. On a six-week trial. Two weeks in, after the Ali versus Spinks rematch, she landed an exclusive interview with Muhammad Ali that nobody else had, and the trial became the job (National Sports Media Association, n.d.).

**Why this is a mechanism and not a nice story.** A trial contract is an employer moving its own risk onto you. Six weeks is not enough time to be gradually accepted; it is only enough time to produce one thing the employer cannot get elsewhere. So the strategy that works is not "perform the role competently." It is "find the asset only you can deliver, and deliver it inside the window."

**What the window is made of.** Access, relationships, language, geography, a beat nobody senior wants. Kennedy's asset was a relationship and a willingness to ask. Every entrant in this course has a version of it, and the ones who lasted knew what theirs was before the contract started.

**The other half of the job.** Kennedy received the National Sports Media Association's Roone Arledge Award for Innovation in 2022, and published a memoir, *Plain Jayne*, in September 2025. That memoir is now the best primary record of her career that exists, which matters because the web's account of her is unusually corrupted. Her Emmy for Rose Parade coverage is real; the year is not settled, so this course does not print one.

:::reveal What does a six-week trial contract actually require of the person taking it? ||| One deliverable the employer cannot obtain from anyone else, inside the window. Gradual competence is not a strategy at six weeks, because there is no time for it to register.

:::reveal Why does this course say "first Black woman to co-anchor a national network sports show" rather than "first woman"? ||| Phyllis George held the same desk before her. The narrower claim is the true one, and a false ordinal discredits everything around it.

## Vocabulary
- **Trial contract**: a short, revocable engagement that shifts hiring risk onto the person being hired.
- **Exclusive**: material a single outlet holds that competitors cannot obtain, which is the unit a trial period is actually judged on.
- **Ordinal claim**: a statement that somebody was first, second, or only, which is true or false and therefore checkable.
- **Primary record**: an account created by the participant or the institution, such as a memoir or an honours list, rather than coverage of coverage.

## Sources
Kennedy Overton, J. (2025). *Plain Jayne*. Andscape Books.

National Sports Media Association. (n.d.). *Jayne Kennedy Overton*. https://nationalsportsmedia.org/news/jayne-kennedy-overton-bio`,
