      title: "10 · Do not argue with the complaints. Do the job.",
      section: "Section 3 · The job has no border",
      body: `Sports broadcasting is a global trade, and the entry problem looks the same in London and Johannesburg as it does in New York. So does the reaction.

**Jacqui Oatley's route.** On April 21, 2007, she commentated on Fulham against Blackburn Rovers for the BBC's *Match of the Day*, the first woman to commentate on that program in its then 43-year history. Two years earlier she had been the first woman to commentate on football for British network radio, on BBC Radio 5 Live. She was appointed MBE in the 2016 New Year Honours for services to broadcasting and diversity in sport. She has since commentated for TNT Sports, anchored darts coverage for ITV, worked as CBS Sports' lead National Women's Soccer League commentator, and in 2022 became the first woman to do play-by-play on the United States World Cup broadcast.

**The reaction was public and it was the story.** Her 2007 debut generated a volume of hostile comment that got covered as news in its own right. What she did with it is the teachable part: she did not spend the following years publicly defending her right to hold the job. She kept commentating, and then built a second career in a second country.

**Why not answering is a strategy rather than a temperament.** Litigating your own legitimacy has three costs. It sets the terms of every conversation about you to a question you did not choose. It converts your working time into unpaid advocacy. And it makes your continued employment look like an argument somebody won, rather than a fact. Output does none of those things. A body of work accumulates whether or not anyone concedes the point.

**Where this advice has limits, said plainly.** "Do the job" is a strategy for hostile commentary, not a substitute for the legal protections that cover harassment, discrimination and retaliation at work. Those are enforceable and they exist for a reason. The point here is narrow: do not mistake winning an argument in public for building a career.

:::reveal What was Jacqui Oatley's first, precisely stated, and on what date? ||| The first woman to commentate on the BBC's *Match of the Day*, on April 21, 2007, on Fulham against Blackburn Rovers, in the program's forty-third year.

:::reveal Name the three costs of publicly litigating your own legitimacy. ||| It lets someone else set the subject of every conversation about you, it converts paid working time into unpaid advocacy, and it makes your employment look like the result of an argument rather than a fact.

## Vocabulary
- **Commentator**: in British usage, the broadcaster calling the action live, equivalent to the American play-by-play announcer.
- **MBE**: Member of the Order of the British Empire, an honour conferred by the sovereign and recorded in a published honours list.
- **Honours list**: the official published record of British honours, which is the primary source for any claim about one.
- **Legitimacy litigation**: spending professional energy arguing that you belong in the role instead of performing it.

## Sources
Fox Sports. (n.d.). *Jacqui Oatley bio*. https://www.foxsports.com/personalities/jacqui-oatley/bio

Yorkshire Post. (n.d.). *Jacqui Oatley on her passion for football, getting an MBE and her first Match of the Day commentary*. https://www.yorkshirepost.co.uk/sport/football/jacqui-oatley-on-her-passion-for-football-getting-an-mbe-and-her-first-match-of-the-day-commentary-1794882`,
