      title: "16 · Come in knowing what you are owed",
      section: "Section 4 · The ladder nobody shows you",
      body: `The third route in is to buy yourself leverage before you arrive, with credentials that look, from outside, like the wrong ones.

**Bianca Smith's route.** Dartmouth College in 2012, in sociology, where she played softball. Then a J.D. and an M.B.A. from Case Western Reserve University in 2017, where she also served as director of baseball operations. Baseball operations internships, including with the Cincinnati Reds. In January 2021 the Boston Red Sox hired her as a minor-league coach at the organization's Fort Myers player development complex, making her the first Black woman to coach in professional baseball (MLB, 2021; ESPN, 2021).

**Two corrections that matter.** Her degrees are from Case Western Reserve, not the University of Cincinnati; the confusion almost certainly comes from her internship with the Reds. And the precise wording of her first is "first Black woman to coach in professional baseball," which is what MLB and the wire coverage used, not "first in MLB history": she coached in the minor-league system, not for the major-league club.

**Why a law degree is a baseball credential.** Minor-league and development-league work is where pay is most informal, rates are least documented, and the rules of the job are least written down. A person arriving with legal and business training arrives already able to read the contract in front of her, price her own labor against something, and recognize when a practice is customary rather than required. That is leverage bought in advance, and it is available to anyone in any undocumented trade who takes the trouble to learn the paperwork of their own field.

**And then she left.** She declined an offer to stay with the Red Sox in 2023. She has since coached in Japan through the JET Programme as a sports exchange advisor in Hokkaido, and has coached Great Britain's women's and under-23 national baseball teams. Her stated goal is to manage in the major leagues. Leaving on her own terms is itself the lesson: the credential stack that got her in also gave her somewhere else to go.

:::reveal Why does this course use "first Black woman to coach in professional baseball" rather than "first in MLB history"? ||| She coached in the Red Sox minor-league system rather than for the major-league club, and the narrower wording is what MLB and the wire coverage used.

:::reveal What does a law and business background actually buy a coach in a minor-league job? ||| The ability to read her own contract, price her labor against something documented, and tell the difference between a practice that is customary and one that is required, in a part of the industry where pay and rules are least written down.

## Vocabulary
- **Player development complex**: an organization's training facility where minor-league players and staff are based, not a named affiliate club.
- **Professional baseball**: the full professional structure including the minor leagues, which is broader than Major League Baseball itself.
- **Informal pay**: compensation set by custom and negotiation rather than by a published scale, which favors whoever knows the market.
- **Leverage bought in advance**: qualifications acquired before entry specifically so that the first negotiation is not conducted from ignorance.

## Sources
ESPN. (2021, January 4). *Boston Red Sox hire Bianca Smith, first Black woman to coach in professional baseball*. https://www.espn.com/mlb/story/_/id/30656231/boston-red-sox-hire-bianca-smith-first-black-woman-coach-professional-baseball

MLB. (2021, January 4). *Red Sox hire Bianca Smith, first Black woman to coach in pro baseball*. https://www.mlb.com/news/red-sox-hire-bianca-smith-first-black-female-baseball-coach`,
